.TH "BASHCLIENT" "1" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server commands"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
bashclient \-s PATH \-a TOKEN [\-e COMMAND | \-f SCRIPT | \-i] [OPTIONS]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]bashclient\f[R] is a standalone command\-line client that connects
to a running \f[B]bash\-server\f[R](1) instance over a Unix domain
socket.
It authenticates using a shared token, then executes commands in one of
three modes: single\-command evaluation, script file execution, or
interactive REPL.
.PP
The client communicates using the v1 (line\-oriented text) protocol.
Each command is sent as an \f[CR]EVAL\f[R] request; the server responds
with zero or more \f[CR]STDOUT\f[R] and \f[CR]STDERR\f[R] lines carrying
base64\-encoded output, followed by an \f[CR]EXIT\f[R] line with the
command\(cqs exit status.
.PP
If no mode flag (\f[B]\-e\f[R], \f[B]\-f\f[R], \f[B]\-i\f[R]) is given
and standard input is a terminal, \f[B]bashclient\f[R] defaults to
interactive mode.
.PP
\f[B]bashclient\f[R] is a self\-contained executable that links only
against \f[B]cygwin1.dll\f[R] \(en it does not depend on
\f[B]cygbash\-5.1.dll\f[R] or any Bash libraries.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-s\f[R], \f[B]\(ensocket\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]
Path to the \f[B]bash\-server\f[R] Unix domain socket.
Required.
.TP
\f[B]\-a\f[R], \f[B]\(enauth\f[R] \f[I]TOKEN\f[R]
Authentication token.
Required.
This is the hex\-encoded 256\-bit token generated by
\f[B]bash\-server\f[R] at startup and stored in
\f[I]<socket\-path>\f[R].token.
.TP
\f[B]\-e\f[R], \f[B]\(eneval\f[R] \f[I]COMMAND\f[R]
Evaluate \f[I]COMMAND\f[R] in the server\(cqs Bash environment, print
its output, and exit.
The client\(cqs exit status is the command\(cqs exit status.
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R], \f[B]\(enfile\f[R] \f[I]SCRIPT\f[R]
Read \f[I]SCRIPT\f[R] line by line and evaluate each non\-empty,
non\-comment line as a separate command.
Lines beginning with \f[CR]#\f[R] are skipped.
The client\(cqs exit status is the exit status of the last command
executed.
.TP
\f[B]\-i\f[R], \f[B]\(eninteractive\f[R]
Enter interactive mode.
Presents a \f[CR]bashclient>\f[R] prompt and reads commands from
standard input.
Type \f[CR]exit\f[R] or \f[CR]quit\f[R] (or press Ctrl\-D) to
disconnect.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\(enverbose\f[R]
Print diagnostic messages, including unexpected server responses.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\(enhelp\f[R]
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-V\f[R], \f[B]\(enversion\f[R]
Print version information and exit.
.SH PROTOCOL
\f[B]bashclient\f[R] uses the v1 text protocol exclusively.
The command exchange sequence is:
.IP
.EX
Client:  AUTH <token>
Server:  OK

Client:  EVAL <command>
Server:  STDOUT <base64\-data>    (zero or more)
Server:  STDERR <base64\-data>    (zero or more)
Server:  EXIT <code>

Client:  PING
Server:  PONG

Client:  QUIT
Server:  BYE
.EE
.PP
All messages are LF\-terminated.
Carriage returns in received data are stripped.
Output payloads (STDOUT, STDERR) are base64\-encoded to preserve binary
data and newlines.
.PP
Error responses take the form \f[CR]ERR <message>\f[R].
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]BASH_SERVER_SOCKET\f[R]
Can be used by wrapper scripts to supply the socket path.
Note that \f[B]bashclient\f[R] itself currently requires the explicit
\f[B]\(ensocket\f[R] flag.
.TP
\f[B]BASH_SERVER_TOKEN\f[R]
Can be used by wrapper scripts to supply the authentication token.
Note that \f[B]bashclient\f[R] itself currently requires the explicit
\f[B]\(enauth\f[R] flag.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]<socket\-path>\f[R].token
Token file written by \f[B]bash\-server\f[R](1).
Wrapper scripts typically read this file and pass its contents via
\f[B]\(enauth\f[R].
